{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Final rebar-debug report\n\nProblem: cumulative observability counters acknowledged their offsets before CloudWatch accepted the corresponding datapoint, permanently dropping the failed interval.\n\nProven root cause: all seven streams across six code shapes persisted the observed total before the matching aws cloudwatch put-metric-data call. Because the offset is the acknowledgement cursor, a transient publish failure made the next run compute zero from an already-advanced baseline. The affected sites were replication, voter, merge-change, deploy, the shared deferral/interrupt helper, and G2P in infra/scripts/observability.sh.\n\nProof: whole-script PATH-shim experiments started each stream at offset 2/source total 7 and forced the targeted AWS call to exit 23. Before repair every stream advanced to 7 and the retry published 0; retaining 2 made the retry publish 5. The reset control reproduced 7 then 0 from offset 10/source 7. Removing only stderr/error suppression did not recover the count, ruling out suppression as a second cause.\n\nTest: tests/scripts/test_observability_offsets_6a65.py was observed RED before repair through the real script, GREEN after repair, and RED again when the exact pre-acknowledgement mutation was restored. After restoring the fix, 10 focused observability tests pass.\n\nFix: Gerrit 1566, commit e701e053a28d244ee639804d501c5dc6198b2d65, merged after LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, and zero unresolved comments. Each stream now advances its offset only inside the successful AWS branch. AWS failure remains best-effort for the systemd probe, but the count remains unacknowledged for retry.\n\nScope and safety: caller sweep found no consumer relying on premature acknowledgement. The change restores the original epic contracts that observed failures publish metrics and trip alarms, adds no dependency, preserves exit-zero, reset, and zero-delta behavior, and weakens no existing test. make lint and make typecheck pass.\n\nSiblings: the construct sweep found and fixed all seven live streams, including the previously omitted G2P stream and both shared-helper metrics. Parity census: CLI/MCP/library surfaces are N/A for this shell probe; automated systemd/autodeploy callers share the repaired script; the read/write dual direction is N/A beyond source-counter reads and CloudWatch publication.\n\nResidual risk and prevention: fixed in scope by the parameterized whole-script regression across every stream. No additional observed fragility remains undispositioned.\n\nReplication: for every targeted stream, a forced first publish failure leaves the offset byte-identical, the second successful run republishes the accumulated delta, and only then advances the offset. The original report no longer reproduces. Ticket intentionally remains in_progress per session directive."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786303034743689001,"uuid":"32961542-1d37-4055-a93b-71174dc3400b"}